\label{def:reachable-directed}


Let $G$ be a simple graph or a simple directed graph. A vertex $v$ is \emph{reachable}
from $u$ if some walk realized in $G$ runs from $u$ to $v$. In the undirected case
reachability is an equivalence relation on $\V{G}$; in the directed case it is only a
preorder, since walks cannot be reversed.

\label{lem:bfs-termination}



If $L_k \neq \emptyset$ then $k < |\V{G}|$. Hence the search from $s$ has exhausted every
layer after $|\V{G}|$ rounds.

\label{thm:bfs-correct}



The set $V_{|\V{G}|}$ is exactly the set of vertices reachable from $s$, and the least
$k < |\V{G}|$ with $v \in L_k$ (or $\infty$ if there is none) is exactly $\dist(s, v)$.
